(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-infancy-adolescence-nutrition-quiz"]={"title":"Nutrition from Infancy Through Adolescence Quiz (Set 1) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture, how does an infant's body weight typically change during the first year?","choices":["It doubles by 1 year and triples by 2 years","It triples by 6 months and quadruples by 1 year","It stays about the same for the first year","It doubles by 4 to 6 months and triples by 1 year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: weight doubles by 4 to 6 months and triples by 1 year of age. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The lecture states an infant's length increases by roughly what amount by the end of the first year?","choices":["About 50%","About 10%","About 100%","About 200%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: length increases about 50% by the end of the first year. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"On a growth chart, what does a child's percentile represent?","choices":["The percentage of body weight that is fat","The percentage of calories from fat in the diet","The number of teeth the child has erupted","The child's rank among 100 peers matched for age and gender"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a percentile is the rank among 100 peers of the same age and gender, with the 50th percentile considered average. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The lecture cautions against overfeeding infants because excess intake can do what?","choices":["Permanently raise the child's metabolic rate","Improve organ development","Reduce future appetite","Increase the number of adipose (fat) cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: overfeeding increases the number of adipose cells, which can persist as obesity develops. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what fat-intake range is appropriate for children ages 1 to 3 years?","choices":["5% to 10% of calories","30% to 40% of calories","10% to 15% of calories","50% to 60% of calories"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: children 1 to 3 years old have a recommended fat-intake range of 30% to 40% of calories. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The lecture identifies which factor as the biggest environmental risk for the undernutrition that leads to failure to thrive?","choices":["Overuse of iron-fortified formula","Introducing solids at 6 months","Poverty","Excess dietary fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: poverty is described as the biggest environmental risk factor for undernutrition leading to failure to thrive. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"The lecture notes infants have high energy needs partly because of a higher body surface area, which causes what?","choices":["Slower metabolism","Reduced need for protein","Lower fluid requirements","Greater heat loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a higher body surface area leads to greater heat loss, contributing to high energy needs. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"For the first 6 months of life, the lecture states that breast milk or formula is adequate nutrition even though it contains what?","choices":["No fiber","No protein","No fat","No carbohydrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: breast milk or formula, containing no fiber, is adequate nutrition for the first 6 months. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Why does the lecture warn against a high-protein diet for infants?","choices":["It prevents absorption of breast milk","Excess nitrogen and minerals can exceed the infant's immature kidney abilities","Protein cannot be digested before 1 year of age","It causes the number of fat cells to fall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a high-protein diet stresses the kidneys because excess nitrogen and minerals exceed the infant's immature kidney abilities. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"The lecture emphasizes that dietary fat is vital in infancy mainly for what reason?","choices":["It lowers the infant's calorie needs","It is vital to nervous system development","It replaces the need for protein","It prevents all food allergies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: fat is vital to nervous system development, and about half of breast-milk energy comes from fat. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"According to the lecture, why is vitamin K routinely given as an injection at birth?","choices":["It cannot be absorbed from formula","Infants are born with dangerously high levels","Intestinal bacteria have not yet begun to synthesize it","Breast milk contains toxic amounts of it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: vitamin K is given at birth because intestinal bacteria only begin to synthesize it afterward. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"The American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation cited in the lecture calls for how much vitamin D per day for all infants and children under 1 year?","choices":["100 international units per day","1,000 international units per day","No vitamin D is recommended in infancy","400 international units per day"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: infants and children under 1 year are recommended 400 international units of vitamin D per day. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"The lecture notes an infant's iron stores are typically depleted by what age, prompting iron-fortified foods or supplementation?","choices":["10 to 12 months","18 to 24 months","4 to 6 months","1 to 2 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: iron stores are depleted by 4 to 6 months of age, prompting iron-fortified formula, supplements, or foods. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"According to the lecture, when is fluoride supplementation NOT recommended for infants?","choices":["Before 6 months of age","After 1 year of age","Only during breastfeeding","Only in hot climates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: fluoride supplementation is not recommended before 6 months, and fluoride-free water should be used in formula before then. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"The lecture advises that supplemental water is NOT recommended during which period, even in hot climates?","choices":["Only for formula-fed infants","During the first 6 months","During the first 2 years","Only after solid foods begin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: supplemental water is not recommended during the first 6 months, even in hot climates. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 156"},{"q":"According to the lecture, up to what share of the total calories in breast milk comes from fat?","choices":["Nearly 0%","Up to about 55%","About 10%","About 35% to 40%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: breast milk provides up to about 55% of total calories as fat, which is calorie-dense. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 180"},{"q":"The lecture states cow's milk is not suitable for infants for which reason?","choices":["Its protein is too high and its mineral content is not suitable","It contains no fat at all","It is too low in every nutrient","It contains added fluoride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cow's milk is not tolerated because its protein is too high and its mineral content is unsuitable for infants. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 191"},{"q":"The lecture lists signs an infant is satiated during feeding. Which set matches?","choices":["Turning the head away, falling asleep, or becoming playful","Crying loudly and rooting","Clenching fists and stiffening","Sucking faster on the bottle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: signs of satiety include turning the head away, falling asleep, or becoming playful. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 205"},{"q":"At about what age does the lecture say an infant is generally ready to begin table foods, added to (not replacing) human milk or formula?","choices":["About 2 months","About 12 months","About 18 months","About 6 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: infants are generally ready for table food at about 6 months, added to milk or formula. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 216"},{"q":"When introducing solid foods, the lecture recommends waiting how long before introducing a new food to check for intolerance?","choices":["About 30 days","No waiting is needed","About 7 days","About 1 hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: introduce one food at a time and wait about 7 days before adding a new food to check for intolerance. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 243"},{"q":"The lecture warns against giving honey or corn syrup to infants because they may contain what?","choices":["Too much fluoride","Lactose","Clostridium botulinum","Excess iron"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: honey and corn syrup are avoided because they may contain Clostridium botulinum. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 288"},{"q":"According to the lecture, until what age should cow's milk NOT be given to an infant?","choices":["Until 1 year of age","Until 6 months of age","Until 2 years of age","Cow's milk is fine from birth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cow's milk should not be given until 1 year of age. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 288"},{"q":"The lecture explains that letting an infant continue with a bottle of carbohydrate-rich fluid promotes dental caries because bacteria do what?","choices":["Block fluoride from reaching the teeth","Increase saliva production","Secrete acids that dissolve tooth enamel","Directly consume the tooth's calcium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: bacteria secrete acids that dissolve enamel when teeth are bathed in carbohydrate-rich fluid. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 268"},{"q":"The lecture states iron-deficiency anemia is most likely to appear in children of what age?","choices":["6 to 24 months","Newborn to 1 month","8 to 12 years","Only in teenagers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: iron-deficiency anemia is most likely to appear in children ages 6 to 24 months as gestational iron stores run out. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 310"},{"q":"According to the lecture, whole milk is recommended for its extra fat energy up to what age, after which reduced-fat or fat-free is preferred?","choices":["Up to 6 months of age","Up to 5 years of age","Up to 10 years of age","Up to 2 years of age"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: whole milk is recommended up to 2 years for extra fat energy, after which reduced-fat or fat-free is preferred. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 320"},{"q":"The lecture notes a wary toddler may need how many exposures before a new food becomes acceptable?","choices":["Foods are never accepted once refused","10 or more exposures","Exactly 1 exposure","Exactly 2 exposures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: it may take 10 or more exposures before a new food is accepted. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 357"},{"q":"For childhood constipation, the lecture notes it may result from an intolerance to which food and recommends more fiber and fluids?","choices":["Fresh fruit","Cow's milk","Iron-fortified cereal","Breast milk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: childhood constipation may result from intolerance to cow's milk, and more fiber, fluids, and activity are recommended. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 391"},{"q":"According to the lecture, roughly what percentage of obese adolescents go on to become obese adults?","choices":["About 80%","About 10%","About 25%","About 50%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: about 80% of obese adolescents become obese adults, versus about 40% of obese children. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 438"},{"q":"Which set correctly lists foods among the 8 leading food allergens in the United States identified in the lecture?","choices":["Apples, bananas, and berries","Milk, eggs, peanuts, and tree nuts","Corn, rice, and potatoes","Beef, chicken, and pork"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: milk, eggs, peanuts, and tree nuts are among the 8 leading allergens, along with fish, crustacean shellfish, wheat, and soybeans. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 643"},{"q":"The lecture cites an American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation to limit children's screen time to about how much?","choices":["About 6 hours per day","There is no recommended limit","Less than 2 hours per day","Less than 8 hours per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the lecture cites limiting screen time to less than 2 hours per day, about 14 hours per week. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 448"}]};
